      "text": "To determine the domain of the function $u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$, we must consider the restrictions on the variable $x$. The square root of $x$, $\\sqrt{x}$, is defined only for non-negative values of $x$. In other words, $x$ must be greater than or equal to zero. Thus, we cannot use values of $x$ that are less than zero.\n\nTo see why, let's consider what it means for a number to be in the denominator of a fraction and if it is in the radicand (inside the square root) of another term or expression. In this case, if the denominator, $\\sqrt{x}$, were zero at $x$, the function $u(x)$ would be undefined, since we cannot divide by zero. Thus, the radicand, $x$, must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $u(x)$ is all real numbers greater than or equal to zero. In interval notation, this is written as:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, \\infty)}\n\\]",
