    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\), we need to consider the constraints on the input \\( x \\). Specifically, since the expression under the square root must be positive, we need \\( x > 0 \\).\n\nLet's write a Python code to ensure the function's validity for a range of values of \\( x \\).\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable and function\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nu = 1 / sp.sqrt(x)\r\n\r\n# Find the domain of the function by ensuring the expression under the square root is strictly greater than 0\r\ndomain = sp.solve(sp.sqrt(x) > 0, x)\r\nprint(domain)\n```\n```output\n(0 < x) & (x < oo)\n```\nThe solution confirms our reasoning. The domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\n\\]\n\nThis indicates that \\( x \\) can take any value from 0 to infinity, excluding 0.",
