    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "\\begin{align*}u(x)&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt x}\\\\\\hline=\\frac{1}{1}\\cdot\\sqrt x-1\\\\\\hline<\\sqrt x\\color{#AAAA77}{\\boxed{\\text{True}}}&=1+\\sqrt x\\\\<1+\\sqrt x\\color{#AAAA77}{\\boxed{\\text{True}}}&=\\sqrt x\\\\<\\sqrt x\\cdot\\sqrt x&=\\sqrt x\\\\\\sqrt x\\cdot\\sqrt x&>1\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\Leftrightarrow\\\\x&>1\\end{align*}The domain notation would be $\\boxed{(1,\\infty)}$, as $x$ cannot be $less than or equal to $0$, or the square root would be undefined. To determine the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\), we need to ensure that the expression inside the square root is positive and that the denominator is not zero. Here is the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the condition for the denominator:**\n   The denominator of the function is \\( \\sqrt{x} \\). For the function to be defined, \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) must be defined and non-zero. Since the square root function is only defined for non-negative numbers and is zero at zero, \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) is defined for \\( x \\geq 0 \\).\n\n2. **Combine the conditions:**\n   Since \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) is the \\( x \\) term under the square root, it must be positive for \\( x \\) to be defined in the denominator and for the function to be defined. Therefore, \\( x \\) must be greater than zero.\n\n3. **Express the domain in interval notation:**\n   The domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is all \\( x \\) such that \\( x > 0 \\). In interval notation, this is written as \\( (0, \\infty) \\).\n\nThus, the domain of the function is \\(\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\\).",
